From: =?UTF-8?q?Dawid=20Wr=C3=B3bel?= <me@dawidwrobel.com>
Date: Fri, 7 Aug 2026 12:36:19 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 41/41] Documentation: fixes

Three corrections to Documentation/tee/qseecom.rst, folded together.

Application lifetime gets a section of its own. The document still said closing
the load session unloads the application, and that client sessions only
reference one, which is the ownership model the driver replaced with reference
counting: there is no owner, every session holds a reference, and the last one
released unloads. The amdtee precedent is given with the reason -- a crash
cannot strand an application it never owned -- and the known limitation is
restated against the same model.

Why this is not part of the QTEE driver is answered where a reader asks it.
QTEE is two SCM calls and an object model advertising TEE_GEN_CAP_OBJREF,
QSEECOM is six operations and a named-application command model advertising
none, and a tee_device carries one impl_id and one set of capabilities. The SCM
plumbing they share is already shared.

The CVE references were wrong. CVE-2019-14041 is a buffer overrun while
processing a listener's modified response, not a race, and it is the path this
driver implements rather than an unrelated Android-kernel bug. CVE-2019-14040
is a use-after-free inside QSEE itself. CVE-2018-9411 is not about reaching the
character device and is dropped. CVE-2021-1961 is described as what it is, an
unchecked offset length while updating a buffer value.

